Brief title: PET/CT-Based Intratumoral Heterogeneity and Multimodal Imaging for Tumor Diagnosis and Prognosis

Official title: Application of 18F-FDG PET/CT Multimodal Metabolic-Anatomical Features and Intratumoral Heterogeneity in Tumor Diagnosis, Staging, and First-Line Therapy Prognostic Stratification: A Multicenter Retrospective and Prospective Cohort Study

Brief summary: Background & Rationale: Intratumoral heterogeneity (ITH) is a recognized hallmark and critical driver of therapeutic resistance, rapid disease progression, and poor survival across solid malignancies, notably advanced non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). While tissue biopsies provide histopathologic confirmation, they are invasive and subject to severe spatial sampling bias. Standard whole-tumor radiomics from single-modality CT often masks localized subregional ecological niches. 18F-FDG PET/CT unites glucose metabolic activity with anatomical resolution, offering an ideal non-invasive tool to map whole-tumor spatial and functional heterogeneity in vivo.

Methods & Design: In this multicenter, bidirectional (retrospective and prospective) observational cohort study, 900 advanced NSCLC patients receiving first-line systemic therapies (chemotherapy, anti-PD-1 immunotherapy, and EGFR-TKIs) were enrolled. Baseline 18F-FDG PET/CT scans obtained within 2 weeks prior to therapy were partitioned into distinct biological habitats using entropy-intensity clustering. IBSI-compliant radiomic features were extracted to develop treatment-specific imaging intratumoral heterogeneity (IITH) signatures. Diagnostic accuracy for staging/metastases and prognostic performance for 6-month progression status, progression-free survival (PFS), and overall survival (OS) were comprehensively validated across internal, external multicenter, and independent prospective cohorts, with biological explainability benchmarked against digital histopathology (HoVer-Net nuclear morphometrics) and transcriptomics.

Detailed description: This cohort study employs an ambispective design, with both retrospective and prospective phases.

Conditions: Solid Neoplasms; Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer

Keywords: PET/CT; 18F-FDG; Intratumoral Heterogeneity; Radiomics; Habitat Imaging; Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer; Tumor Diagnosis; Prognosis; Progression-Free Survival; Prospective and Retrospective Cohort Study

Intervention (OTHER): This is an observational study with no intervention.. This is an observational study with no intervention.

primaryOutcome: 6-Month Progression-Free Survival [PFS] Status. Based on the Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors (RECIST v1.1), the risk probability score derived from the baseline 18F-FDG PET/CT multimodal intratumoral heterogeneity model is adopted to predict the binary endpoint of disease progression (PD) or all-cause death within 6 months after patients receive first-line systemic anti-tumor therapy. This binary endpoint evaluates 6 months PFS status in patients.

secondaryOutcome: Progression-Free Survival. 

secondaryOutcome: Overall Survival. 

secondaryOutcome: Diagnostic Accuracy for Nodal and Distant Metastases. 

Eligibility criteria: Inclusion Criteria:

* Aged 18-79 years, male or female;
* Confirmed primary solid malignant tumor (non-small cell lung cancer) by histopathological or cytological examination, clinically staged as stage III-IV according to the 8th edition of AJCC/UICC staging system;
* Planned to receive or receiving first-line standard systemic therapy (including chemotherapy, anti-PD-1/PD-L1 immune checkpoint inhibitors, or EGFR-TKI targeted therapy);
* Standardized baseline 18F-FDG PET/CT examination completed within 2 weeks before the first anti-tumor treatment, with complete whole-body/thoracic metabolic parameters (SUVmax, MTV, etc.) and thin-slice enhanced/non-enhanced CT image sequences; images with satisfactory quality and without severe respiratory motion artifacts;
* At least one measurable solid tumor target lesion per RECIST v1.1;
* Complete baseline clinicopathological characteristics, baseline laboratory data, and standardized long-term follow-up records (including progression-free survival and overall survival status);
* Written informed consent signed by the patient or legal representative (waiver of informed consent for the retrospective part has been approved by the institutional review board).

Exclusion Criteria:

* Unclear pathological diagnosis, non-primary malignant tumor, or concomitant active second primary malignant tumor at other sites;
* Prior surgical resection, radical radiotherapy, or systemic anti-tumor therapy (neoadjuvant therapy, conversion therapy, etc.) targeting the primary lesion before baseline PET/CT examination;
* Severe uncontrolled systemic metabolic diseases (e.g., severe diabetes with fasting blood glucose > 11.1 mmol/L that significantly interferes with 18F-FDG uptake distribution), severe hepatic or renal insufficiency, or severe infection that renders the patient intolerant to examination and follow-up;
* Incomplete PET/CT imaging data, contrast medium injection failure, extremely low image signal-to-noise ratio, or severe respiratory motion artifacts/intracorporeal metal implant artifacts which prevent accurate lesion delineation and radiomic analysis;
* Severe missing of key clinical follow-up data, loss to follow-up, or inability to accurately determine objective treatment response and survival endpoints.

Brief title: Multimodal Liquid-Biopsy Omics Biomarkers of Treatment Response in Breast Cancer

Official title: Exploration of Treatment-Response Biomarkers in Breast Cancer Based on Integrated Liquid-Biopsy Multimodal Omics Features

Brief summary: This single-center cohort study integrates humoral immune response profiling (Phage ImmunoPrecipitation Sequencing, PhIP-seq) with circulating cell-free DNA (cfDNA) genomic and epigenomic features, exosome transcriptomics, and plasma metabolomics to discover and validate liquid-biopsy biomarkers of treatment response in breast cancer.

The study comprises four cohorts: (1) a retrospective pan-cancer cohort of approximately 1,000 patients with solid malignant tumor, using archived residual serum/plasma to construct a pan-cancer antigen repertoire and cfDNA mutational/methylation atlas, with priority given to patients who had available Whole Exome Sequencing (WES) data; (2) a prospective cohort of 800 women with non-metastatic (M0) breast cancer receiving standard neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NAC); (3) a prospective cohort of 100 women with metastatic (M1) breast cancer receiving standard systemic therapy; and (4) a control group of 100 individuals, including patients with benign breast disease (breast fibroadenoma) and healthy volunteers.

The primary objective is to identify biomarkers-autoantibody repertoires, cfDNA mutation clearance, methylation reprogramming, exosome transcripts, and metabolic signatures-associated with pathological complete response (pCR) to NAC, and to develop predictive models where feasible.

Detailed description: Peripheral blood (20 mL) is collected at protocol-defined time points. Serum/Plasma autoantibody repertoires are profiled by PhIP-seq(phage-display immunoprecipitation sequencing); cfDNA methylation is measured by bisulfite-free methylome profiling; exosome RNA is profiled by strand-specific RNA sequencing; and untargeted plasma metabolomics is performed by liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry. The study endpoints include clinical response and pathological complete response after NAC, together with survival follow-up. The study is conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki and Chinese regulations for investigator-initiated research, and has been approved by the institutional ethics committee.

Ambispective - a retrospective pan-cancer cohort (archived samples, 2020 Jan - 2026. Sep) combined with prospective breast cancer cohorts (enrollment 2026. Oct -2029)

Conditions: Lung Cancer Non-small Cell; Colorectal Cancer; Stomach Cancer; Hepatocellular Carcinoma; Breast Cancer

Keywords: Liquid biopsy; PhIP-seq; Neoadjuvant chemotherapy; cfDNA methylation; Pathological complete response

primaryOutcome: Pathological Complete Response (pCR) Rate. Proportion of patients achieving pCR after completion of neoadjuvant chemotherapy, defined as ypT0/is ypN0 (no invasive cancer in the breast and ipsilateral axillary lymph nodes; ductal carcinoma in situ may be present), consistent with RCB-0.

secondaryOutcome: Residual Cancer Burden (RCB) Class. 

secondaryOutcome: Invasive Disease-Free Survival (iDFS). 

secondaryOutcome: Progression-Free Survival (PFS). 

secondaryOutcome: Overall Survival (OS). 

Eligibility criteria: Inclusion Criteria:

Retrospective pan-cancer cohort:

1. Aged 18 to 80 years;
2. Both genders
3. Clinically and histopathologically confirmed diagnosis of solid malignant tumors, including but not limited to non-small cell lung cancer, colorectal cancer, gastric cancer, hepatocellular carcinoma, or breast cancer.
4. Complete clinical data available, including definitive TNM stage and established treatment plan;
5. Archived peripheral blood samples (serum/plasma) collected during routine clinical care.

Prospective neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NAC) breast cancer cohort:

1. Female, aged 18 to 80 years (inclusive);
2. Clinically and pathologically confirmed non-metastatic (M0) breast cancer;
3. Planned standard NAC at the study site, with complete pre-treatment ER, PR, and HER2 immunohistochemistry results available;
4. Voluntary participation with signed written informed consent.

Prospective metastatic breast cancer cohort:

1. Female, aged 18 to 80 years (inclusive);
2. Clinically and pathologically confirmed metastatic (M1) breast cancer;
3. Planned standard systemic therapy at the study site, with complete ER, PR, and HER2 immunohistochemistry results available;
4. Voluntary participation with signed written informed consent.

Benign/Healthy control group:

1. Healthy female volunteers, or female patients with breast fibroadenoma undergoing minimally invasive biopsy during the same period;
2. Voluntary participation with signed written informed consent.

Exclusion Criteria:

For prospective breast cancer cohorts:

1. History of mastitis, or systemic rheumatic/autoimmune diseases (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis);
2. Acute or chronic infectious diseases within the past 6 months (e.g., pneumonia, pulmonary tuberculosis, active hepatitis);
3. Long-term use of glucocorticoids, immunosuppressants, biologic agents, or psychotropic medications (short-term or as-needed use excepted);
4. Inability or refusal to provide informed consent.
